rocco on December 20, 2016, after it was restored. A government grant of about $844,000 funded the restorations, according to the Maghreb Arab Press.","wpu":"Ettedgui Synagogue","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/%D9%88%D8%A7%D8%AC%D9%87%D8%A9_%D9%83%D9%86%D9%8A%D8%B3_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AA%D8%AF%D8%BA%D9%8A_%D9%81%D9%8A_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AF%D8%A7%D8%B1_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%B6%D8%A7%D8%A1.jpg/960px-%D9%88%D8%A7%D8%AC%D9%87%D8%A9_%D9%83%D9%86%D9%8A%D8%B3_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AA%D8%AF%D8%BA%D9%8A_%D9%81%D9%8A_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AF%D8%A7%D8%B1_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%B6%D8%A7%D8%A1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Ould el-Hamra Mosque","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":33.6015,"lo":-7.61834,"d":390,"ds":"Ould el-Hamra Mosque (Arabic: مسجد ولد الحمرا) is a mosque in the medina of Casablanca first erected in 1789. Along with the Ettedgui Synagogue and the Church of San Buenaventura, it is of the three buildings representing the three Abrahamic religions in a space of 250 m2 in the medina.","wpu":"Ould el-Hamra Mosque","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Jam%C3%A0a_weld_el_hamara_%C3%A0_casablanca.jpg/960px-Jam%C3%A0a_weld_el_hamara_%C3%A0_casablanca.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Cinema Rialto","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":33.5941,"lo":-7.6144,"d":1257,"ds":"Cinema Rialto (Arabic: سينما ريالطو) is a movie theater built in 1929 in Casablanca, Morocco. It is one of the oldest cinemas in Morocco.","wpu":"Cinema Rialto","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Cin%C3%A9ma_Rialto_%C3%A0_Casablanca_le_19_juillet_2026_-_Photo_Bertrand_SOUBEYRAND.jpg/960px-Cin%C3%A9ma_Rialto_%C3%A0_Casablanca_le_19_juillet_2026_-_Photo_Bertrand_SOUBEYRAND.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Central Market (Casablanca)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":33.594706,"lo":-7.612006,"d":1272,"ds":"The Central Market (Arabic: السوق المركزي, French: Marché Central) in Casablanca, Morocco is a marketplace with historical and cultural significance. It is located on Muhammad V Boulevard, among the colonial architecture of the 20th century, facing the Central Market Tramway Station.","wpu":"Central Market (Casablanca)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/%CE%9A%CE%B5%CE%BD%CF%84%CF%81%CE%B9%CE%BA%CE%AE_%CE%91%CE%B3%CE%BF%CF%81%CE%AC_%CE%9A%CE%B1%CE%B6%CE%B1%CE%BC%CF%80%CE%BB%CE%AC%CE%BD%CE%BA%CE%B1_1821.jpg/960px-%CE%9A%CE%B5%CE%BD%CF%84%CF%81%CE%B9%CE%BA%CE%AE_%CE%91%CE%B3%CE%BF%CF%81%CE%AC_%CE%9A%CE%B1%CE%B6%CE%B1%CE%BC%CF%80%CE%BB%CE%AC%CE%BD%CE%BA%CE%B1_1821.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Temple Beth-El (Casablanca)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":33.596817,"lo":-7.62965,"d":1411,"ds":"The Temple Beth-El (Hebrew: בית הכנסת בית־אל, romanized: Béit hak'néset Bēṯ-ʾEl, lit. 'Synagogue of the House of God'; Arabic: معبد بيت إيل, romanized: Maʿbad bayt ʾĪl, lit. 'Temple of the House of El') also called Beit-El or the Algerian Temple, is an Orthodox Jewish congregation and synagogue in the Sidi Belyout district of Casablanca, Morocco.","wpu":"Temple Beth-El (Casablanca)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/%D9%85%D8%B9%D8%A8%D8%AF_%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%AA_%D8%A5%D9%8A%D9%84.jpg/960px-%D9%85%D8%B9%D8%A8%D8%AF_%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%AA_%D8%A5%D9%8A%D9%84.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Casablanca Cathedral","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":33.591111,"lo":-7.624444,"d":1655,"ds":"The Casablanca Cathedral (Arabic: كاتدرائية الدار البيضاء, French: Cathédrale de Casablanca), or Church of the Sacred Heart (Arabic: كنيسة القلب المقدس, French: Église du Sacré-Cœur), is a former church located west of the Arab League Park in Casablanca, Morocco.","wpu":"Casablanca Cathedral","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/f/f1/Cath%C3%A9drale_Casablanca.jpg/960px-Cath%C3%A9drale_Casablanca.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Arab League Park","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":33.588611,"lo":-7.624167,"d":1909,"ds":"The Arab League Park (Arabic: حديقة جامعة الدول العربية, romanized: Ḥadīqat Jāmiʻat al-Duwal al-ʻArabīyah) is an urban park in Casablanca, Morocco. It is located in the center of the city, west of Hassan II Boulevard, east of Roudani Boulevard and Algiers Street, and south east of the Church of the Sacred Heart. The park covers 30 acres of land, and is bisected by Moulay Youssef Boulevard.","wpu":"Arab League Park","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Arab_League_Park%2C_Casablanca%3B_%D8%AD%D8%AF%D9%8A%D9%82%D8%A9_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AC%D8%A7%D9%85%D8%B9%D8%A9_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D8%B1%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%A9%D8%8C_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AF%D8%A7%D8%B1_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%B6%D8%A7%D8%A1.jpg/960px-Arab_League_Park%2C_Casablanca%3B_%D8%AD%D8%AF%D9%8A%D9%82%D8%A9_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AC%D8%A7%D9%85%D8%B9%D8%A9_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D8%B1%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%A9%D8%8C_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AF%D8%A7%D8%B1_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%B6%D8%A7%D8%A1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Casablanca Twin Center","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":33.586111,"lo":-7.631944,"d":2465,"ds":"The Casablanca Twin Center (Arabic: برجا الدار البيضاء) is a complex of two skyscrapers located at Casablanca, Morocco. The two structures, the West Tower and the East Tower, have 28 floors each. The center houses a complex of shops, offices, and a five-star hotel, and lies at the heart of Casablanca in the Maarif district, at the crossroads between Zerktouni Boulevard and the Boulevard Al Massira Al Khadra.","wpu":"Casablanca Twin Center","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/f/ff/Twin_Center_2.jpg/960px-Twin_Center_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Notre-Dame de Lourdes Church (Casablanca)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":33.58275,"lo":-7.615806,"d":2482,"ds":"Notre-Dame de Lourdes Church (French: Église Notre-Dame-de-Lourdes de Casablanca) is a Roman Catholic church in modernist style in Casablanca, Morocco. It was built in 1954 by architect Achille Dangleterre and engineer Gaston Zimmer.","wpu":"Notre-Dame de Lourdes Church (Casablanca)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/6/6f/Eglise_N.D._de_Lourdes%2C_Casablanca%2C_Dioc%C3%A8se_de_Rabat%2C_Maroc.jpg/960px-Eglise_N.D._de_Lourdes%2C_Casablanca%2C_Dioc%C3%A8se_de_Rabat%2C_Maroc.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Royal Palace of Casablanca","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":33.5804,"lo":-7.6055,"d":2970,"ds":"The Royal Palace of Casablanca (Arabic: القصر الملكي بالدار البيضاء, romanized: Al-Qaṣr al-Malakī bi-al-Dār al-Bayḍāʼ) is the main royal residence of the King of Morocco in Casablanca, Morocco. Located in the Hubous neighborhood, it was built in the 1920s on a design by the brothers Louis-Paul and Félix-Joseph Pertuzio, with garden landscaping by Jean-Claude Nicolas Forestier.","wpu":"Royal Palace of Casablanca","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Casablanca_MohammedVI_Palace.jpg/960px-Casablanca_MohammedVI_Palace.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Al-Quds Mosque (Casablanca)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":33.599389,"lo":-7.583389,"d":3265,"ds":"Al-Quds Mosque (Arabic: مسجد القدس, Berber: ⵎⴻⵣⴳⵉⴷⴰ ⵍⵇⵓⴷⵙ), formerly Église de Sainte Marguerite, is a mosque in the Roches Noires neighborhood of Casablanca, Morocco. It was originally built as a church built in a Neo-Gothic style, but it was converted into a mosque after Morocco's independence.","wpu":"Al-Quds Mosque (Casablanca)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/%D9%83%D9%86%D9%8A%D8%B3%D8%A9_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B5%D8%AE%D9%88%D8%B1_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B3%D9%88%D8%AF%D8%A7%D8%A1_%D9%81%D9%8A_%D9%85%D8%AF%D9%8A%D9%86%D8%A9_%D9%83%D8%A7%D8%B2%D8%A7%D8%A8%D9%84%D8%A7%D9%86%D9%83%D8%A7_%D9%88%D9%87%D9%8A_%D8%A3%D8%B5%D8%A8%D8%AD%D8%AA_%D9%85%D8%B3%D8%AC%D8%AF_%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%82%D8%AF%D8%B3.jpg/960px-%D9%83%D9%86%D9%8A%D8%B3%D8%A9_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B5%D8%AE%D9%88%D8%B1_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B3%D9%88%D8%AF%D8%A7%D8%A1_%D9%81%D9%8A_%D9%85%D8%AF%D9%8A%D9%86%D8%A9_%D9%83%D8%A7%D8%B2%D8%A7%D8%A8%D9%84%D8%A7%D9%86%D9%83%D8%A7_%D9%88%D9%87%D9%8A_%D8%A3%D8%B5%D8%A8%D8%AD%D8%AA_%D9%85%D8%B3%D8%AC%D8%AF_%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%82%D8%AF%D8%B3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Al-Mohammadi Mosque","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":33.576306,"lo":-7.606056,"d":3377,"ds":"Al-Mohammadi Mosque (Arabic: مسجد المحمدي) is a large mosque in the Habous quarter of Casablanca, Morocco. It was built circa 1935 and its construction was sponsored by Sultan Mohammed V, after whom it is named.","wpu":"Al-Mohammadi Mosque","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Al_mohamedia_Mosque_in_Habous_district_Casablanca_the_mosque_was_built_by_Mohamed_the_fifth_king_of_morocco.jpg/960px-Al_mohamedia_Mosque_in_Habous_district_Casablanca_the_mosque_was_built_by_Mohamed_the_fifth_king_of_morocco.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"El Hank Lighthouse","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":33.61,"lo":-7.6547,"d":3443,"ds":"The El Hank lighthouse is a lighthouse located on the tip of El Hank, west of the port of Casablanca. It is managed by the Port and Maritime Authority within the Ministry of Equipment, Transport, Logistics and Water.","wpu":"El Hank Lighthouse","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Casablanca_Lighthouse.jpg/960px-Casablanca_Lighthouse.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Assunna Mosque","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":33.561739,"lo":-7.609353,"d":4876,"ds":"Assunna Mosque (Arabic: مسجد السُّنَّة, Berber: ⵎⴻⵣⴳⵉⴷⴰ ⴰⵙⵓⵏⴰ) is a mosque designed by Jean-François Zevaco in a modernist architectural style in Casablanca, Morocco in 1966. It features brutalist architectural elements such as raw concrete. It is located at the junction of Blvd.","wpu":"Assunna Mosque","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/2/25/Patio_with_minaret_view_-_mosque_Assuna%2C_Casablanca%2C_Morocco.jpg/960px-Patio_with_minaret_view_-_mosque_Assuna%2C_Casablanca%2C_Morocco.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Museum of Moroccan Judaism","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":33.5557,"lo":-7.6424,"d":5929,"ds":"The Museum of Moroccan Judaism (Arabic: متحف اليهودية المغربية, Hebrew: מוזיאון יהדות מרוקו) is a museum of Jewish life in Morocco in Casablanca, Morocco. Established in 1997, it became the first museum devoted to Judaism in the Arab world. The museum, whose building originated in 1948 as a Jewish orphanage that housed up to 160 Jewish youth, was opened by King Mohammed VI of Morocco on 20 December 2016.","wpu":"Museum of Moroccan Judaism","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Moroccan_Jewish_Museum%2C_Casablanca_Morocco.jpg/960px-Moroccan_Jewish_Museum%2C_Casablanca_Morocco.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Casablanca Finance City Tower","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":33.5635,"lo":-7.661,"d":6095,"ds":"Casablanca Finance City Tower (Arabic: برج القطب المالي للدار البيضاء, romanized: Burj al-Quṭb al-Mālī lldār al-Bayḍāʼ) also known as the CFC Tower is a 28–story office building in Casablanca, Morocco. Built between 2016 and 2019, the tower stands at 136 m (446 ft) tall and is the fourth tallest building in Morocco. It is located in the Casablanca Finance City, built at the site of the former Casablanca–Anfa Airport.","wpu":"Casablanca Finance City Tower","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/c/c6/CFC_Tower_Casa.jpg/960px-CFC_Tower_Casa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Casablanca is the largest city in Morocco and the country's economic and business centre. Located on the Atlantic coast of the Chaouia plain in the central-western part of Morocco, the city has a population of about 3.22 million in the urban area, and over 4.27 million in Greater Casablanca, making it the most populous city in the Maghreb region, and the ninth-largest in the Arab world. The Port of Casablanca is one of the largest artificial ports in Africa, and the third-largest port in North Africa, after Tanger-Med (40 km or 25 mi east of Tangier) and Port Said.","wpu":"Casablanca","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Casa_finance_city_6_%28cropped%29.jpg/960px-Casa_finance_city_6_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}